{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And the people thirsted there, etc.</b> Now the matter gives rise to astonishment: why did HaShem bring them into this great trial, to die of thirst? According to reason a person would suppose that this and similar things would breed denial. And the difficulty grows further regarding Moses: in a place where he should gird his loins with prayer and supplication, he came instead with a complaint before HaShem: ״עוֹד מְעַט״ etc. — so that it would appear he was not concerned about their thirst and about the death of the children of Israel by thirst, for the verse testifies that they thirsted for water." "<b>And it shall be seen</b> that HaShem tested them in order to train them to lift their eyes and pray before HaShem, for this is a great fundamental principle in faith and in the perfection of the soul. And you will find that HaShem acted wisely in the matter and did not give them manna for a month at a time but rather day by day; and therefore He withheld the miracle from them until they would supplicate before their Maker and He would hear their cry. And the verse informs us that in the place where they should have cried out to HaShem, they instead quarreled with Moses; and when thirst overpowered them, ״the people complained against Moses.״ Therefore the verse sets forth the order of events: ״they thirsted, etc., and complained,״ etc. — to say that they did nothing other than this: from this you learn that they did not cry out to HaShem. And so the verse testified concerning their trial, etc.: ״Is HaShem in our midst or not?״ — from this you learn that they did not affirm [the truth of] divine providence. For the Master, blessed be He, was compelling them to cry out to Him and to remove the evil thought, through the force of the thirst; and all this Moses understood and grasped, and therefore he did not pray to HaShem but was expecting the Master's hope from them. And when he saw that the pain was great, he said before HaShem <b>What shall I do for this people</b> — meaning: what shall I do for them to remove their complaint from upon me. And from this you know that Moses sensed that this matter did not depend on prayer, for otherwise he would have prayed as he did at the sea, and even if he were not answered he would have persisted in prayer until HaShem answered him. And he further argued before HaShem <b>a little more</b> etc. — meaning: if You add to their distress a little more, the inevitable outcome that will come from them is ״they will stone me״; and even if after that You add to their distress until they cry out before You with great weeping and the intention of HaShem is fulfilled, nevertheless in the meantime ״Moses goes and gets stoned״ — and this is an argument that is heard." "And I saw fit to ponder how the people of HaShem, who saw wondrous and awesome things in Egypt and at the sea and in the wilderness, could err in the matter and not cry out to HaShem — when they were the ones who recognized the benefit of crying out, for ״He heard their groaning in Egypt״ (Exodus 2:23). And perhaps it is because their reasoning was that there is no need for prayer over the matter so long as HaShem is present among them, for after He brought them into the wilderness it is obvious that He did not bring them except to provide their needs, for if not, they are surely lost and would not need prayer; rather, [their reasoning was that] the fact [that they are suffering] means that HaShem is not in their midst — and this is the trial about which they said: ״Is HaShem in our midst״ etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:16.281397937Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}